course: dictionary
lesson: 15289
title: One word, several meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, so you must look at the words around them to find the right one. The context tells you which meaning fits. «Yes, sire,» said the servant to the king. Sire is a polite way of speaking to a king, like «Your Majesty».
explain: In horse racing and dog breeding, the sire is the father of the animal. «The puppy's sire won first prize at the dog show» means the puppy's father won first prize. The mother is called the dam.
explain: Sire can also be a verb, which means to be the father of a young animal. «The stallion sired three foals this year.» Check by swapping in your meaning: the stallion was the father of three foals. It still makes sense, so you have it right.
fact: Context means the words and sentences around a word.
fact: «Sire» can be a polite way of speaking to a king: «Yes, sire.»
fact: The sire of a horse or dog is its father.
fact: To «sire» means to be the father of a young animal.
fact: «Sire» is also an old word for a lord or a father, and it is mostly found in old stories.
exercise: «Yes, sire,» said the servant to the king. What does sire mean here? | A stone wall | * A polite way of speaking to a king | A river | A kind of horse
exercise: «The puppy's sire won first prize at the dog show.» Who is the sire? | The owner's brother | The puppy's mother | The judge at the show | * The puppy's father
exercise: Find the word in «The stallion sired three foals this year.» that means was the father of. | * sired | foals | stallion | year
test: «The knight bowed low and said, "Good morning, sire."» What does sire mean here? | A horse that wins races | * A polite way to speak to a king | A dark forest | A type of boat
test: Ella says the sire of a horse is its mother. What is the correct answer? | * The sire is the father | The sire is the mother | The sire is the foal | The sire is the owner
exam: «The old racehorse sired many champions.» What does sired mean? | * Was the father of | Trained at dawn | Sold for money | Raced against
